1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,353
The police can search your car without a

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:03,154
warrant.

3
00:00:03,155 --> 00:00:05,600
They've been able to for a hundred years —

4
00:00:05,601 --> 00:00:08,280
with one exception.

5
00:00:08,281 --> 00:00:09,680
You get pulled over.

6
00:00:09,681 --> 00:00:12,093
The officer walks up, glances through the

7
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:14,453
glass, and says he's going to search the

8
00:00:14,454 --> 00:00:15,254
car.

9
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:16,740
Can he?

10
00:00:16,741 --> 00:00:17,541
When?

11
00:00:17,542 --> 00:00:20,280
And where, exactly, does that power run out?

12
00:00:20,281 --> 00:00:22,575
The answer stretches from a Prohibition

13
00:00:22,576 --> 00:00:25,020
bootlegger on a Michigan highway to a

14
00:00:25,021 --> 00:00:27,820
motorcycle hidden under a tarp in a Virginia

15
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:28,621
driveway —

16
00:00:28,622 --> 00:00:31,151
and it quietly decides how much privacy you

17
00:00:31,152 --> 00:00:33,840
actually keep the moment you turn the key.

18
00:00:33,841 --> 00:00:36,370
Because there is one place they still can't

19
00:00:36,371 --> 00:00:37,171
go —

20
00:00:37,172 --> 00:00:39,467
and in 2018 the Supreme Court drew that

21
00:00:39,468 --> 00:00:43,540
line at the very edge of your home.

22
00:00:43,541 --> 00:00:46,620
Start with the thing that surprises almost

23
00:00:46,621 --> 00:00:47,421
everyone.

24
00:00:47,422 --> 00:00:49,600
To search your house, the police usually

25
00:00:49,601 --> 00:00:50,542
need a warrant —

26
00:00:50,543 --> 00:00:53,073
a judge's signature, probable cause written

27
00:00:53,074 --> 00:00:55,074
down, a name for the place and the

28
00:00:55,075 --> 00:00:56,520
thing they're after.

29
00:00:56,521 --> 00:00:59,680
That is the Fourth Amendment at full

30
00:00:59,681 --> 00:01:00,481
strength.

31
00:01:00,482 --> 00:01:02,070
Your car does not get that.

32
00:01:02,071 --> 00:01:04,200
And it hasn't since 1925 —

33
00:01:04,201 --> 00:01:06,320
a hundred years ago.

34
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:08,638
It was Prohibition.

35
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,228
Alcohol was illegal, and a whole underground

36
00:01:11,229 --> 00:01:13,641
economy ran it across state lines by car.

37
00:01:13,642 --> 00:01:15,820
A man named George Carroll was one of

38
00:01:15,821 --> 00:01:18,056
those runners, moving liquor along the

39
00:01:18,057 --> 00:01:20,800
highway between Detroit and Grand Rapids.

40
00:01:20,801 --> 00:01:22,977
Federal agents had been watching him.

41
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:25,449
They stopped his car, and they didn't find

42
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:26,921
anything in plain sight —

43
00:01:26,922 --> 00:01:28,804
so they started tearing into it.

44
00:01:28,805 --> 00:01:30,864
They ripped open the seat cushions.

45
00:01:30,865 --> 00:01:33,159
And hidden behind the upholstery of the

46
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,631
seats, they pulled out sixty-eight bottles

47
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:37,060
of whisky and gin.

48
00:01:37,061 --> 00:01:39,520
Carroll's lawyers had what sounds like a

49
00:01:39,521 --> 00:01:41,900
winning argument: no warrant, no search.

50
00:01:41,901 --> 00:01:44,019
The agents had torn his car apart on

51
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:46,137
the side of the road with nothing on

52
00:01:46,138 --> 00:01:46,920
paper.

53
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:48,980
The case climbed all the way to the

54
00:01:48,981 --> 00:01:49,804
Supreme Court.

55
00:01:49,805 --> 00:01:51,960
And the Court gave an answer that has

56
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:53,961
governed your glovebox ever since.

57
00:01:53,962 --> 00:01:54,903
It drew a line —

58
00:01:54,904 --> 00:01:58,200
not around what the police wanted,

59
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,518
but around the thing being searched.

60
00:02:01,519 --> 00:02:03,140
A house, the Court said,

61
00:02:03,141 --> 00:02:03,941
stays put.

62
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:06,001
If officers have to leave and get a

63
00:02:06,002 --> 00:02:08,960
warrant, they can post someone at the door;

64
00:02:08,961 --> 00:02:10,785
the house isn't going anywhere.

65
00:02:10,786 --> 00:02:12,374
But a car is built to move.

66
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:14,257
By the time a warrant is signed,

67
00:02:14,258 --> 00:02:15,058
a car —

68
00:02:15,059 --> 00:02:17,236
and whatever is hidden in its seats —

69
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:20,040
can be in another county,

70
00:02:20,041 --> 00:02:21,217
another state, gone.

71
00:02:21,218 --> 00:02:23,513
Chief Justice Taft wrote that you can't

72
00:02:23,514 --> 00:02:24,867
treat the two the same,

73
00:02:24,868 --> 00:02:27,279
"because the vehicle can be quickly moved

74
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,869
out of the locality or jurisdiction in which

75
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:31,517
the warrant must be sought."

76
00:02:31,518 --> 00:02:34,280
So the Court carved out what we now

77
00:02:34,281 --> 00:02:36,046
call the automobile exception.

78
00:02:36,047 --> 00:02:38,400
If police have probable cause to believe

79
00:02:38,401 --> 00:02:40,636
your car contains contraband, they can

80
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:42,220
search it —

81
00:02:42,221 --> 00:02:43,633
right there, no warrant.

82
00:02:43,634 --> 00:02:45,520
And this isn't a dusty relic.

83
00:02:45,521 --> 00:02:47,815
That 1925 rule is still the rule behind

84
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,261
why an ordinary traffic stop can,

85
00:02:50,262 --> 00:02:52,792
under the right facts, become an open trunk

86
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,084
on the shoulder of the highway.

87
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:57,180
Not everyone agreed at the time.

88
00:02:57,181 --> 00:03:00,200
Justice McReynolds, joined by Justice

89
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:02,114
Sutherland, dissented.

90
00:03:02,115 --> 00:03:03,000
But the majority held —

91
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:05,060
and here's the phrase to hold onto,

92
00:03:05,061 --> 00:03:07,620
because the entire story turns on it.

93
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:08,920
Probable cause.

94
00:03:08,921 --> 00:03:09,745
Not a warrant.

95
00:03:09,746 --> 00:03:10,569
Not certainty.

96
00:03:10,570 --> 00:03:11,923
But not a hunch either.

97
00:03:11,924 --> 00:03:16,260
We're going to see exactly where they bite.

98
00:03:16,261 --> 00:03:19,140
Here's the part movies and TV get wrong.

99
00:03:19,141 --> 00:03:21,435
The automobile exception is not a blank

100
00:03:21,436 --> 00:03:22,236
check.

101
00:03:22,237 --> 00:03:23,708
Being pulled over is not,

102
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:26,120
by itself, permission to search your car.

103
00:03:26,121 --> 00:03:28,460
And a hunch is not enough.

104
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,220
Probable cause means a fair,

105
00:03:31,221 --> 00:03:33,500
fact-based probability that there's

106
00:03:33,501 --> 00:03:35,325
contraband or evidence inside —

107
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:37,540
something an officer can point to and

108
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:39,365
articulate, not just a feeling.

109
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,067
A busted taillight does not,

110
00:03:41,068 --> 00:03:43,380
on its own, get anyone into your trunk.

111
00:03:43,381 --> 00:03:45,460
So what does clear the bar?

112
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:48,040
The smell of something illegal in some

113
00:03:48,041 --> 00:03:48,841
places.

114
00:03:48,842 --> 00:03:51,313
Something an officer sees in plain view on

115
00:03:51,314 --> 00:03:52,114
the seat.

116
00:03:52,115 --> 00:03:53,060
An admission.

117
00:03:53,061 --> 00:03:54,860
A trained dog's alert.

118
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:55,920
Facts that add up.

119
00:03:55,921 --> 00:03:57,820
And once that line is crossed —

120
00:03:57,821 --> 00:04:00,180
once there really is probable cause —

121
00:04:00,181 --> 00:04:03,100
the search can go much further than people

122
00:04:03,101 --> 00:04:03,901
expect.

123
00:04:03,902 --> 00:04:06,140
Because the Supreme Court has said the reach

124
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:08,317
of the search is defined by the thing

125
00:04:08,318 --> 00:04:09,480
they're looking for.

126
00:04:09,481 --> 00:04:11,280
If the object could fit in the glovebox,

127
00:04:11,281 --> 00:04:12,820
they can open the glovebox.

128
00:04:12,821 --> 00:04:14,040
If it could be in the trunk,

129
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:15,453
they can open the trunk.

130
00:04:15,454 --> 00:04:17,454
If it could be inside a zipped bag

131
00:04:17,455 --> 00:04:19,920
on the back seat, they can open the

132
00:04:19,921 --> 00:04:20,721
bag.

133
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:23,180
Locked container, loose container —

134
00:04:23,181 --> 00:04:25,357
if that's a place the thing could be,

135
00:04:25,358 --> 00:04:26,241
it's fair game.

136
00:04:26,242 --> 00:04:28,477
That reach didn't come out of nowhere.

137
00:04:28,478 --> 00:04:30,537
It was settled in two Supreme Court

138
00:04:30,538 --> 00:04:31,338
decisions —

139
00:04:31,339 --> 00:04:32,620
United States v.

140
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:34,740
Ross in 1982, and California v.

141
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:36,200
Acevedo in 1991 —

142
00:04:36,201 --> 00:04:38,142
which held that a closed bag or a

143
00:04:38,143 --> 00:04:40,555
locked box inside the car gets no special

144
00:04:40,556 --> 00:04:41,880
shield of its own.

145
00:04:41,881 --> 00:04:44,234
If the thing they have probable cause to

146
00:04:44,235 --> 00:04:45,588
find could be in there,

147
00:04:45,589 --> 00:04:47,200
they can open it.

148
00:04:47,201 --> 00:04:49,436
But that same rule cuts the other way,

149
00:04:49,437 --> 00:04:51,860
and it's the closest thing you have to

150
00:04:51,861 --> 00:04:52,661
a leash.

151
00:04:52,662 --> 00:04:54,780
The search has to fit the reason for

152
00:04:54,781 --> 00:04:55,581
it.

153
00:04:55,582 --> 00:04:57,876
If the probable cause is about a stolen

154
00:04:57,877 --> 00:05:00,465
fifty-inch television, that does not justify

155
00:05:00,466 --> 00:05:02,113
opening a tiny pill bottle —

156
00:05:02,114 --> 00:05:03,780
a TV can't hide in there.

157
00:05:03,781 --> 00:05:06,460
The bigger the thing they're chasing,

158
00:05:06,461 --> 00:05:08,755
the fewer the places they're allowed to

159
00:05:08,756 --> 00:05:09,556
look.

160
00:05:09,557 --> 00:05:11,616
The smaller it is, the more of your

161
00:05:11,617 --> 00:05:12,417
car opens up.

162
00:05:12,418 --> 00:05:14,771
They don't even have to prove a separate

163
00:05:14,772 --> 00:05:15,480
emergency, though.

164
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:17,140
The Court has been blunt about it: if

165
00:05:17,141 --> 00:05:19,494
the car is drivable and there's probable

166
00:05:19,495 --> 00:05:21,240
cause, that's enough —

167
00:05:21,241 --> 00:05:22,712
no extra excuse required.

168
00:05:22,713 --> 00:05:25,640
The Justices spelled that out again in the

169
00:05:25,641 --> 00:05:28,560
nineteen-nineties: a car that's readily

170
00:05:28,561 --> 00:05:31,660
mobile, plus probable cause, needs nothing

171
00:05:31,661 --> 00:05:32,461
more.

172
00:05:32,462 --> 00:05:33,462
No ticking clock.

173
00:05:33,463 --> 00:05:35,698
No proof the driver was about to speed

174
00:05:35,699 --> 00:05:36,020
off.

175
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:38,197
The car being a car is the emergency.

176
00:05:38,198 --> 00:05:40,434
Now step back and feel what that means

177
00:05:40,435 --> 00:05:41,235
for you.

178
00:05:41,236 --> 00:05:43,900
Your car is where you keep your bag,

179
00:05:43,901 --> 00:05:46,254
your phone, your paperwork, half of your

180
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,340
private life while it's in motion.

181
00:05:48,341 --> 00:05:50,812
And it carries far thinner protection than

182
00:05:50,813 --> 00:05:51,871
your living room —

183
00:05:51,872 --> 00:05:54,440
for a reason written down before your

184
00:05:54,441 --> 00:05:55,794
grandparents were born.

185
00:05:55,795 --> 00:05:58,324
Which raises the only question that's left.

186
00:05:58,325 --> 00:05:59,972
If the car is this exposed —

187
00:05:59,973 --> 00:06:04,140
is there anywhere the police still cannot

188
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:04,960
follow it?

189
00:06:04,961 --> 00:06:06,961
To find the line, go to Virginia —

190
00:06:06,962 --> 00:06:09,860
and a motorcycle that kept getting away.

191
00:06:09,861 --> 00:06:12,155
An officer had twice been outrun by the

192
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:14,360
same distinctive orange-and-black bike, and

193
00:06:14,361 --> 00:06:15,949
he suspected it was stolen.

194
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:17,950
Then, one day, he tracked it down.

195
00:06:17,951 --> 00:06:19,420
Not on a street.

196
00:06:19,421 --> 00:06:20,221
To a house.

197
00:06:20,222 --> 00:06:22,940
And there it was: parked in the driveway,

198
00:06:22,941 --> 00:06:24,941
pulled up close to the side of the

199
00:06:24,942 --> 00:06:27,660
home, with a tarp thrown over it.

200
00:06:27,661 --> 00:06:29,896
So the officer walked up the driveway.

201
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,398
He lifted the tarp.

202
00:06:31,399 --> 00:06:33,810
He ran the plate and the vehicle number —

203
00:06:33,811 --> 00:06:35,223
and it came back stolen.

204
00:06:35,224 --> 00:06:37,860
Then he waited for the man staying there,

205
00:06:37,861 --> 00:06:40,000
Ryan Collins, and arrested him.

206
00:06:40,001 --> 00:06:42,780
Here's why this case is the whole ballgame.

207
00:06:42,781 --> 00:06:45,220
The officer had real reason to think that

208
00:06:45,221 --> 00:06:46,600
bike was stolen —

209
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:47,895
that's probable cause.

210
00:06:47,896 --> 00:06:50,367
And if that motorcycle had been sitting on

211
00:06:50,368 --> 00:06:53,440
a public street, lifting the tarp is a

212
00:06:53,441 --> 00:06:56,100
clean, legal, automobile-exception search.

213
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,780
So Virginia argued: same thing here.

214
00:06:58,781 --> 00:07:00,940
A vehicle, probable cause, done —

215
00:07:00,941 --> 00:07:03,300
it shouldn't matter that it was in a

216
00:07:03,301 --> 00:07:04,101
driveway.

217
00:07:04,102 --> 00:07:06,573
But think about how much that position was

218
00:07:06,574 --> 00:07:07,632
really asking for.

219
00:07:07,633 --> 00:07:09,869
To search a car parked at a stranger's

220
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:12,700
public curb, an officer needs nothing but

221
00:07:12,701 --> 00:07:13,583
probable cause.

222
00:07:13,584 --> 00:07:16,055
Virginia wanted that same power to include

223
00:07:16,056 --> 00:07:17,879
walking onto private property —

224
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,320
up the driveway, past the point where a

225
00:07:20,321 --> 00:07:21,121
home begins.

226
00:07:21,122 --> 00:07:23,710
If that were allowed, the most exposed thing

227
00:07:23,711 --> 00:07:26,500
you own, your car, could become the key

228
00:07:26,501 --> 00:07:29,100
that unlocks the most protected place you

229
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:30,940
have, your home.

230
00:07:30,941 --> 00:07:34,100
The Supreme Court said no.

231
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:35,920
In 2018, by a vote of eight to

232
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:38,215
one, the Court held that the car-search

233
00:07:38,216 --> 00:07:40,480
power does not reach onto the ground that

234
00:07:40,481 --> 00:07:41,716
belongs to your home.

235
00:07:41,717 --> 00:07:43,541
There's a word for that ground.

236
00:07:43,542 --> 00:07:45,240
The porch, the front steps,

237
00:07:45,241 --> 00:07:48,120
the strip of driveway pressed against the

238
00:07:48,121 --> 00:07:48,921
walls —

239
00:07:48,922 --> 00:07:50,746
the law calls it the curtilage.

240
00:07:50,747 --> 00:07:53,041
And curtilage is treated as part of the

241
00:07:53,042 --> 00:07:53,820
home.

242
00:07:53,821 --> 00:07:55,880
It gets the home's full protection.

243
00:07:55,881 --> 00:07:57,646
And that isn't a technicality.

244
00:07:57,647 --> 00:08:00,176
The home is the ground the Fourth Amendment

245
00:08:00,177 --> 00:08:01,412
guards most fiercely.

246
00:08:01,413 --> 00:08:04,001
The Court's point was simple: you cannot let

247
00:08:04,002 --> 00:08:06,238
a rule built for the open road quietly

248
00:08:06,239 --> 00:08:08,709
swallow the most protected ground a person

249
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:09,140
owns.

250
00:08:09,141 --> 00:08:11,520
Where you park does not shrink your home.

251
00:08:11,521 --> 00:08:13,880
Your home enlarges what it protects.

252
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:16,552
Justice Sotomayor, writing for the Court,

253
00:08:16,553 --> 00:08:18,847
said it in one line worth keeping: "The

254
00:08:18,848 --> 00:08:24,020
scope of the automobile exception extends no

255
00:08:24,021 --> 00:08:26,139
further than the automobile itself."

256
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,022
So both things are true at once.

257
00:08:28,023 --> 00:08:30,258
Out on the street, that same search is

258
00:08:30,259 --> 00:08:30,920
fine.

259
00:08:30,921 --> 00:08:33,156
But the instant the car is tucked into

260
00:08:33,157 --> 00:08:35,510
the protected ground hugging your house,

261
00:08:35,511 --> 00:08:38,099
the hundred-year exception runs out of road.

262
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:40,218
To step onto that ground and search,

263
00:08:40,219 --> 00:08:42,480
police need a warrant —

264
00:08:42,481 --> 00:08:44,680
or a completely different exception.

265
00:08:44,681 --> 00:08:46,446
Only Justice Alito dissented —

266
00:08:46,447 --> 00:08:49,000
worried the Court was letting a technicality

267
00:08:49,001 --> 00:08:51,177
shield a bike he had strong reason to

268
00:08:51,178 --> 00:08:52,296
believe was stolen.

269
00:08:52,297 --> 00:08:53,540
But he stood alone.

270
00:08:53,541 --> 00:08:54,365
The line held.

271
00:08:54,366 --> 00:08:56,840
So now you know where the line falls.

272
00:08:56,841 --> 00:08:59,312
The question that actually matters is what

273
00:08:59,313 --> 00:09:03,820
it means the next time it's your window

274
00:09:03,821 --> 00:09:05,527
the officer is walking up to.

275
00:09:05,528 --> 00:09:08,320
So here's the actual map of your rights

276
00:09:08,321 --> 00:09:10,080
on the road —

277
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:11,963
the one almost nobody is handed.

278
00:09:11,964 --> 00:09:14,141
On a public street or a routine stop,

279
00:09:14,142 --> 00:09:16,880
your car can be searched without a warrant —

280
00:09:16,881 --> 00:09:18,587
but only with probable cause,

281
00:09:18,588 --> 00:09:21,160
and only as far as the thing they're

282
00:09:21,161 --> 00:09:22,926
looking for could actually be.

283
00:09:22,927 --> 00:09:25,338
Being arrested doesn't automatically open

284
00:09:25,339 --> 00:09:26,222
the car either.

285
00:09:26,223 --> 00:09:27,517
In a case called Gant,

286
00:09:27,518 --> 00:09:29,989
the Court said that once you're handcuffed

287
00:09:29,990 --> 00:09:31,754
and set away from the vehicle,

288
00:09:31,755 --> 00:09:34,049
officers generally can't search it just

289
00:09:34,050 --> 00:09:35,639
because they arrested you —

290
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,780
not unless it's reasonable to think evidence

291
00:09:38,781 --> 00:09:40,840
of that specific offense is inside.

292
00:09:40,841 --> 00:09:43,253
The protection can even follow you into a

293
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:44,200
rental.

294
00:09:44,201 --> 00:09:46,672
The Court has said that if you're lawfully

295
00:09:46,673 --> 00:09:48,860
driving a rental car, you can keep a

296
00:09:48,861 --> 00:09:51,220
real expectation of privacy in it —

297
00:09:51,221 --> 00:09:54,600
even if your name isn't on the paperwork.

298
00:09:54,601 --> 00:09:57,080
And there's one honest gray area worth

299
00:09:57,081 --> 00:09:57,881
naming.

300
00:09:57,882 --> 00:09:59,411
For years, in many places,

301
00:09:59,412 --> 00:10:01,942
the smell of marijuana alone was treated as

302
00:10:01,943 --> 00:10:02,825
probable cause.

303
00:10:02,826 --> 00:10:05,060
But the Supreme Court has never set a

304
00:10:05,061 --> 00:10:07,800
single nationwide rule on that —

305
00:10:07,801 --> 00:10:09,919
and in states that have legalized or

306
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,331
decriminalized it, several courts now say

307
00:10:12,332 --> 00:10:14,685
the odor by itself isn't enough anymore.

308
00:10:14,686 --> 00:10:16,940
On that one, the honest answer is: it

309
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:18,235
depends where you are.

310
00:10:18,236 --> 00:10:20,177
So what does all of this mean the

311
00:10:20,178 --> 00:10:22,414
next time you're actually pulled over?

312
00:10:22,415 --> 00:10:24,560
You don't have to be a lawyer.

313
00:10:24,561 --> 00:10:27,540
Just carry the shape of it: they need

314
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:30,007
a real, articulable reason —

315
00:10:30,008 --> 00:10:30,808
not a mood —

316
00:10:30,809 --> 00:10:33,221
before they can search; the search has to

317
00:10:33,222 --> 00:10:35,457
match the thing they're after; and the

318
00:10:35,458 --> 00:10:38,046
ground pressed against your house is not the

319
00:10:38,047 --> 00:10:38,847
road.

320
00:10:38,848 --> 00:10:41,080
But the throughline is a century old,

321
00:10:41,081 --> 00:10:42,180
and it's simple.

322
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,080
Your car trades privacy for mobility.

323
00:10:45,081 --> 00:10:46,493
The exception is broad —

324
00:10:46,494 --> 00:10:48,800
broader than most people believe —

325
00:10:48,801 --> 00:10:50,340
and it isn't a myth.

326
00:10:50,341 --> 00:10:53,880
It's just not infinite.

327
00:10:53,881 --> 00:10:56,240
It stops, exactly, at the edge of your

328
00:10:56,241 --> 00:10:57,041
home.

329
00:10:57,042 --> 00:10:59,042
If you drive, that line is yours —

330
00:10:59,043 --> 00:11:01,600
and most people go their whole lives never

331
00:11:01,601 --> 00:11:03,440
knowing exactly where it falls.

332
00:11:03,441 --> 00:11:05,912
If knowing where your rights actually stop

333
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:08,800
changed how you'll see the next traffic

334
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:10,161
stop, hit like —

335
00:11:10,162 --> 00:11:12,574
it honestly helps more drivers find this.

336
00:11:12,575 --> 00:11:14,751
And tell me in the comments: were you

337
00:11:14,752 --> 00:11:17,282
taught that police need a warrant to search

338
00:11:17,283 --> 00:11:17,922
your car?

339
00:11:17,923 --> 00:11:19,000
Because for a hundred years,

340
00:11:19,001 --> 00:11:19,501
they haven't.
